
class Solution {
    public String longestCommonPrefix(String[] str) {

        // Sort the array of strings in lexicographical (dictionary) order
        // After sorting, the strings with the smallest and largest prefixes
        // will be at the first and last positions
        Arrays.sort(str);

        // Take the first string (smallest lexicographically)
        String s1 = str[0];

        // Take the last string (largest lexicographically)
        String s2 = str[str.length - 1];

        // Variable to track matching characters index
        int i = 0;

        // Compare characters of s1 and s2 until they stop matching
        while(i < s1.length()){

            // If characters at index i are the same in both strings
            if(s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(i)){
                i++; // move to next character
            }else{
                // Stop when characters differ
                break;
            }
        }

        // If no characters matched, there is no common prefix
        if(i == 0){
            return "";
        }else{
            // Return the substring from start to the last matching index
            return s1.substring(0 , i);
        }
    }
}
💡 Key Idea of This Approach

Sort the array of strings.

Only compare the first and last string.

Because in sorted order, the maximum possible difference occurs between first and last.

The common prefix of these two will be the prefix for the whole array.

⏱ Time Complexity

Sorting: O(n log n)

Comparing characters: O(m)
Where:

n = number of strings

m = length of the shortest string

Example

Input:

["flower","flow","flight"]

After sorting:

["flight","flow","flower"]

Compare:

flight
flower

Common prefix → "fl"

Return:

"fl"
